     "field": "definition",
     "severity": "Medium",
     "issue": "The definition lists 'breaking up standing waves (fixed loud and quiet zones)' as a benefit of diffusion. For a beginner-tagged entry this reinforces a very common misconception - that diffusers fix low-frequency room-mode/bass buildup problems. Diffusion is effective only where the device dimensions are comparable to the wavelength (mid/high frequencies); low-frequency standing waves are addressed with absorption/bass trapping, not diffusion. A learner could mis-apply a diffuser to a boomy/bass problem.",
     "suggestion": "Reframe the benefit around what diffusion actually addresses at the frequencies it works on: 'It helps create natural-sounding spaces while breaking up flutter echo and strong mid/high-frequency reflections.' Drop or qualify the standing-waves claim, and (in common_mistakes) note that low-frequency room modes/bass buildup need absorption or bass trapping, not diffusion."
